{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c722228c7bcb4e139f0984b8a3c22f11","ordinal":1,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.1","text":"When people investigate any subject, the likely outcome is one of three: they find it; or they deny that it can be found and admit that it is inapprehensible; or they keep on investigating."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-052fd7e1cc9d425d84f7f132a566ddcd","ordinal":2,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.2","text":"\n\nThat is perhaps why, with the things investigated in philosophy too, some have said that they have found the truth, others have declared that it cannot be apprehended, and others are still investigating."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-780cdd4a199741fb82bade7daf1e61d1","ordinal":3,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.3","text":"\n\nThose who think they have found it are the dogmatists in the proper sense: Aristotle and his followers, for example, Epicurus and his, the Stoics, and certain others. Clitomachus, Carneades and other Academics have declared that things are inapprehensible. And the skeptics are still investigating.\n〔1〕 Carneades (214–129 BC) and his pupil Clitomachus led the Academy, Plato’s school, in its skeptical phase."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-366366e14a9041998a235105a7585d86","ordinal":4,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.4","text":"\n\nSo it seems reasonable to hold that there are three most basic kinds of philosophy: the dogmatic, the Academic and the skeptical. It will be for others to discuss the first two. For now, we will speak in outline about the skeptical way. But first we should say this: on none of the things we are about to say do we firmly maintain that it is absolutely as we say. We simply report on each item as a chronicler would, according to how it appears to us at the moment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bea0a7aaa4054f1da3fa0fa912b5fa66","ordinal":5,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.5","text":"\n\nThere are two kinds of account of skeptical philosophy, a general one and a specific one. In the general account we set out the character of skepticism. We say what its concept is, what its principles and its arguments are, what its criterion is and what its goal, what the modes of suspension of judgment are, how we take the skeptical statements, and how skepticism is distinguished from the philosophies closest to it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ade4f4bc8f04225aee457e149e2b7ec","ordinal":6,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.6","text":"\n\nIn the specific account we argue against each part of what is called philosophy. Let us first deal with the general account, and begin our exposition with the names of the skeptical way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7a884e6a2a5746aeaed904d35f2950b0","ordinal":7,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.7","text":"\n\nThe skeptical way is called investigative, from its activity of investigating and inquiring. It is called suspensive, from the feeling that comes about in the inquirer after the investigation. It is called aporetic, either, as some say, from its being at a loss and investigating about everything, or from its being unable to choose between assent and denial. And it is called Pyrrhonian, because Pyrrho appears to us to have applied himself to skepticism more fully and more conspicuously than anyone before him.\n〔1〕 The three Greek names come from verbs meaning “to investigate,” “to hold back (judgment)” and “to be at a loss” (aporein); skepsis itself means “inquiry.” Pyrrho of Elis (c. 360–270 BC) was regarded as the founder of the movement."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a396e163662f4cf0a070cda323f118a5","ordinal":8,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.8","text":"\n\nSkepticism is an ability to set out oppositions between what appears and what is thought, in any way whatever. Starting from it, because of the equipollence of the opposed objects and arguments, we come first to suspension of judgment, and after that to tranquility."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e21134854e4e4d098b3bb4ec568d92db","ordinal":9,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.9","text":"\n\nWe call it an “ability” not in any elaborate sense, but simply in the sense of being able to. By “what appears” we here mean objects of perception, which is why we contrast them with objects of thought. “In any way whatever” can be attached to “ability,” so that we take the word “ability” in its simple sense, as we said. It can also be attached to “set out oppositions between what appears and what is thought.” For since we oppose these in various ways—what appears to what appears, what is thought to what is thought, or the one to the other—we say “in any way whatever” so that all the oppositions are included. Or it can be attached to “what appears and what is thought,” so that we don’t investigate how what appears appears or how what is thought is thought, but simply take them as they come."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-54b46f7a2e1b47e4934a2a968596ec5f","ordinal":10,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.10","text":"\n\nBy “opposed” arguments we don’t necessarily mean a denial and an affirmation, but simply arguments that conflict. By “equipollence” we mean equality with respect to being convincing or unconvincing, so that none of the conflicting arguments takes precedence over any other as more convincing. “Suspension of judgment” is a standstill of the intellect, owing to which we neither reject nor posit anything. “Tranquility” is freedom from disturbance and calmness of soul. How tranquility comes along with suspension of judgment we will point out when we discuss the goal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4def6cd16cc4403ba50f6cb69c9c6883","ordinal":11,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.11","text":"\n\nThe Pyrrhonian philosopher, too, has implicitly been accounted for in the concept of the skeptical way: he is the one who possesses this ability."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1b22eb460dbb4b4ca84de2c84e5b9637","ordinal":12,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.12","text":"\n\nWe say that the causal principle of skepticism is the hope of becoming tranquil. People of talent were disturbed by the anomaly in things and at a loss as to which of them they should rather assent to. So they set out to investigate what in things is true and what is false, thinking that by deciding these questions they would become tranquil. The chief principle of the skeptical system is that to every argument an equal argument is opposed. For it is from this, we think, that we end up holding no beliefs."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8558018f7c9645be9cee3bfbd687d7a1","ordinal":13,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.13","text":"\n\nWhen we say that the skeptic does not hold beliefs, we don’t mean “belief” in the more general sense in which some say that belief is simply acquiescing in something. (For the skeptic does assent to the feelings forced on him by an impression: when he is warmed or chilled, for example, he would not say “I think I am not being warmed” or “chilled.”) Rather, we say that he does not hold beliefs in the sense in which some say that belief is assent to some non-evident matter of the kind investigated by the sciences. (For the Pyrrhonian assents to nothing non-evident.)"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fecbc52ff49944a4bc92d195d556315a","ordinal":14,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.14","text":"\n\nNor does he hold beliefs when he utters the skeptical phrases about non-evident matters—“no more (this than that),” for example, or “I determine nothing,” or any of the others we will discuss later. For someone who holds a belief posits as real the thing he is said to hold the belief about. The skeptic, by contrast, does not posit these phrases as absolutely real. He supposes that the phrase “everything is false” says that it too, along with everything else, is false, and similarly with “nothing is true.” In just the same way, “no more” says that it too, along with everything else, is no more so than not, and so it cancels itself along with the others. And we say the same about the other skeptical phrases."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-956757fd4aec405e92f21d89c4baed1e","ordinal":15,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.15","text":"\n\nBesides, if the person who holds beliefs posits as real what he holds a belief about, while the skeptic utters his phrases on the understanding that they are implicitly cancelled by themselves, then he could not be said to hold beliefs in uttering them. Most important of all, in uttering these phrases he says what appears to himself and reports his own feeling without holding opinions, making no firm claim about the external objects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aeca283baff045b49cf0f54d9e1c61ee","ordinal":16,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.16","text":"\n\nWe take a similar line when we are asked whether the skeptic has a school. ⟨For⟩ if someone says that a school is an adherence to many beliefs that are consistent both with one another and with ⟨the⟩ appearances, and says that a belief is assent to something non-evident, then we will say that the skeptic has no school."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0decc5225b7145d1a0bb67ea7c0fba6b","ordinal":17,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.17","text":"\n\nBut suppose someone says that a school is a way of life that follows a certain line of reasoning in accordance with what appears—reasoning that shows how it is possible to seem to live correctly (“correctly” taken not only with reference to excellence, but in a looser sense) and that leads to being able to suspend judgment. Then we say that he has a school. For we follow a line of reasoning that, in accordance with what appears, points us to a life in conformity with our ancestral customs, our laws, our ways of life and our own feelings."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-347e0a534a51483eb6e994fd2b0dd6ac","ordinal":18,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.18","text":"\n\nWe say much the same when it is asked whether the skeptic should do natural science. We do not do natural science in order to make statements with firm conviction about any of the matters on which natural science holds beliefs. But we do take up natural science in order to be able to oppose to every argument an equal argument, and for the sake of tranquility. We approach the logical and the ethical parts of what is called philosophy in the same way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7be2db62ce7a41d389a828604c558909","ordinal":19,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.19","text":"\n\nThose who say that the skeptics do away with appearances seem to me not to have listened to what we say. As we said before, we do not overturn the things that lead us, against our will, to assent in accordance with an impression we are passively affected by; and these are the appearances. When we investigate whether the underlying thing is such as it appears, we grant that it appears. What we investigate is not what appears, but what is said about what appears—and that is different from investigating what appears itself."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-eff00ad163774318b45a95289002fdb7","ordinal":20,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.20","text":"\n\nFor example, honey appears to us to be sweet. This we concede, since we perceive the sweetness. But whether it is also sweet, as far as the argument goes, we investigate; and that is not what appears but ⟨what is⟩ said about what appears. And if we do propound arguments directly against appearances, we set them out not because we want to do away with appearances, but to expose the rashness of the dogmatists. For if reason is such a trickster that it all but snatches even appearances from under our eyes, surely we should be wary of it in non-evident matters, so as not to follow it and be rash?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-240ad4407ab74b558e2b18df51415ad4","ordinal":21,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.21","text":"\n\nThat we attend to appearances is clear from what we say about the criterion of the skeptical way. “Criterion” has two senses. One is the criterion adopted to give conviction about whether something is real or not; we will discuss it in our counter-arguments. The other is the criterion of action: by attending to it in everyday life we do some things and not others. It is this one we are now discussing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7c6c134f42d74885bc4ac91011930fc7","ordinal":22,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.22","text":"\n\nWe say, then, that the criterion of the skeptical way is what appears—implicitly meaning by this the impression. For since the impression lies in our being affected and in involuntary feeling, it is not open to investigation. That is why no one, presumably, will dispute that the underlying thing appears this way or that. What is investigated is whether it is such as it appears."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7b2d66798b7f483fbf273af1917f456a","ordinal":23,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.23","text":"\n\nSo we attend to appearances and live without holding opinions, in accordance with the observances of everyday life, since we cannot be wholly inactive. These observances of everyday life seem to fall into four parts. One lies in the guidance of nature, one in the necessity of feelings, one in the handing down of laws and customs, and one in the teaching of arts. The guidance of nature is"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7a64791e0bf746bdaf94e7a7b23b7fa6","ordinal":24,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.24","text":"\n\nthat by which we are naturally capable of perceiving and thinking. The necessity of feelings is that by which hunger leads us to food and thirst to drink. The handing down of customs and laws is that by which, in everyday life, we accept piety as good and impiety as bad. The teaching of arts is that by which we are not inactive in the arts we take up. But we say all this without holding opinions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9b43fc2624224114b960a12483aeb37e","ordinal":25,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.25","text":"\n\nThe next thing would be to go through the goal of the skeptical way. A goal is that for the sake of which everything is done or considered, while it is not itself for the sake of anything else; or, the final object of desire. We say, so far, that the goal of the skeptic is tranquility in matters of opinion and moderate feeling in matters forced upon us."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a31ed54e82374fd2b8067d5d62e723f5","ordinal":26,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.26","text":"\n\nThe skeptic began to do philosophy in order to decide among impressions and apprehend which are true and which false, so as to become tranquil. But he ran into equipollent disagreement, and since he could not decide it, he suspended judgment. And once he had suspended judgment, tranquility in matters of opinion followed, as it happened."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d0d75df1cbc54a1c9b6f6579f756319b","ordinal":27,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.27","text":"\n\nFor anyone who holds the opinion that something is by nature good or bad is constantly disturbed. When he lacks the things that seem to him good, he thinks he is being hounded by things that are by nature bad, and he chases after what he supposes to be good. Then, once he has acquired these, he falls into still more disturbances. He is elated beyond reason and without measure, and because he fears change he does everything he can so as not to lose the things that seem to him good."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e5a500bb64654b3fb7f12f7bf795645f","ordinal":28,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.28","text":"\n\nBut someone who does not determine anything about what is good or bad by nature neither avoids nor pursues anything intensely, and so he is tranquil. What happened to the skeptic, then, is just what is told of Apelles the painter. They say that he was painting a horse and wanted to depict the horse’s foam. He failed so badly that he gave up and threw at the picture the sponge he used to wipe the paint off his brush—and the sponge, striking the picture, produced a likeness of the horse’s foam.\n〔1〕 Apelles (fourth century BC), court painter to Alexander the Great, was the most celebrated painter of antiquity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fd9b3f9ee3954199a894add33018d3a2","ordinal":29,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.29","text":"\n\nSo too the skeptics were hoping to achieve tranquility by deciding the anomaly in what appears and what is thought. Unable to do this, they suspended judgment. And once they had suspended judgment, tranquility followed, as if by chance, as a shadow follows a body. We do not, however, think that the skeptic is wholly undisturbed. We say that he is disturbed by things forced upon him: we do agree that he is sometimes cold and thirsty, and has various feelings of that sort."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b9daac46b4d748c1a463c89c67ff125a","ordinal":30,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.30","text":"\n\nBut even here ordinary people are afflicted twice over: by the feelings themselves, and no less by thinking that these conditions are by nature bad. The skeptic, by doing away with the additional opinion that each of them is bad by nature, gets off more moderately even here. That is why we say that the goal of the skeptic is tranquility in matters of opinion and moderate feeling in matters forced upon us. Some eminent skeptics have added to these suspension of judgment in investigations."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-634c8ded3e54422a96a1b62347f78391","ordinal":31,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.31","text":"\n\nSince we have said that tranquility follows suspension of judgment about everything, the next thing would be to say how we come to suspend judgment. To put it in rather general terms, it comes about through the opposition of things. We oppose what appears to what appears, or what is thought to what is thought, or the one to the other. We oppose what appears to what appears, for example,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e76b8bfd955748e8a5e458f132527233","ordinal":32,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.32","text":"\n\nwhen we say, “The same tower appears round from a distance but square from close up.” We oppose what is thought to what is thought when, against someone who establishes from the order of the heavenly bodies that there is providence, we set the fact that the good often fare badly and the bad fare well, and conclude from this that there is no providence."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-55ce8d35acd540da97a973549edfb263","ordinal":33,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.33","text":"\n\nWe oppose what is thought to what appears, as Anaxagoras did in reply to ⟨someone who argued⟩ that snow is white: snow is frozen water, and water is black; so snow too is black. On another way of looking at it, we sometimes oppose present things to present things, as in the cases just mentioned, and sometimes present things to past or future ones. For instance, when someone propounds an argument to us that we cannot refute, we say to him:\n〔1〕 Anaxagoras of Clazomenae was a fifth-century BC natural philosopher."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f1ba883d940c43a69ef6343671a916ed","ordinal":34,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.34","text":"\n\n“Before the founder of the school you follow was born, the argument for it did not yet appear sound, though in nature it was already there. In the same way, it is possible that the argument opposed to the one you have just propounded is there in nature but does not yet appear to us. So we should not yet assent to the argument that now seems strong.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-95e4f31d253f45abb3bcf412cbd9afec","ordinal":35,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.35","text":"\n\nBut so that we may grasp these oppositions more precisely, I will also set out the modes through which suspension of judgment is brought about. I make no firm claims about their number or their force: they may be unsound, and there may be more of them than the ones I am about to describe."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-586b04995eae4cc3b038cda4f7f3ff18","ordinal":36,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.36","text":"\n\nThe older skeptics usually hand down modes through which suspension of judgment seems to be brought about, ten in number; they also call them, interchangeably, “arguments” and “patterns.” They are these: first, the mode depending on the variety of animals; second, the one depending on the differences among human beings; third, the one depending on the different constitutions of the sense organs; fourth, the one depending on circumstances; fifth, the one depending on positions, distances and places; sixth, the one depending on admixtures;\n〔1〕 The Ten Modes are usually attributed to Aenesidemus (first century BC), who revived Pyrrhonism."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a5185843ab3e49258e96d382d6db26f0","ordinal":37,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.37","text":"\n\nseventh, the one depending on the quantities and preparations of the underlying things; eighth, the one from relativity; ninth, the one depending on constant or rare encounters; tenth, the one depending on ways of life, customs, laws, mythical beliefs and dogmatic suppositions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ee281ffafa404e05a2df6915a066e5ea","ordinal":38,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.38","text":"\n\nWe use this order by stipulation. Over these there are three higher modes: the one from the judge, the one from what is judged, and the one from both. Under the mode from the judge fall the first four, since the judge is either an animal, or a human being, or a sense, or in some circumstance. The seventh and the tenth ⟨are referred⟩ to the mode from what is judged; and the fifth, sixth, eighth and ninth to the mode composed of both."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-efe1b9bf19254b4a85097c3f4ffd1f78","ordinal":39,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.39","text":"\n\nThese three in turn are referred to the mode of relativity. So relativity is the most generic, the three are specific, and the ten are subordinate. This is what we say about their number, as is plausible. About their force, the following."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-45ed041fa6094fde985ee92afc8c3cc6","ordinal":40,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.40","text":"\n\nThe first argument, we said, is the one according to which, ⟨owing to⟩ the differences among animals, the same things do not produce the same impressions. We infer this both from the differences in the ways animals come into being and from the variation in the composition of their bodies."}]}